From 160224821dbef6d57fd1df4536a92b050885be10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 23:42:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] gxcore: the vertex block as three uniform bindings, each
 staged as far as read

Every draw staged the whole 2832-byte VertexShaderConstants whenever any
of it changed. At sea in Wind Waker a draw's own matrices change in 96
percent of blocks, XF matrix memory in 97, the lights in 2, and most
draws (lit, without matrix indices) read no matrix memory at all.

The block is now three dynamic-offset bindings of group 1, each staged
and de-duplicated on its own: the draw's own fields (vsc: its matrices,
projection, texgen matrices, materials, cached N/B/T; 656 bytes), XF
matrix memory (vsm: transformmatrices and the normal bank; 1536) and the
lights (vsl; 640). The struct's fields are reordered into those parts.
gxc::vertex_uniform_use says how far a key's shader reads each part
(the WGSL declares that much); a part it does not bind is not staged
and keeps any valid offset. The ubershader binds all three in full, so
a draw it may draw stages them whole. In-between frames' blocks go the
same way. gxcore_wgsl_test checks every field each tested shader reads
against vertex_uniform_use.

Uniform upload per frame, same tour: 4154 -> 1341 KB at the busiest
point, 3279 -> 1027 at Dragon Roost, 1307 -> 653 in Hyrule's room
(-50 to -69 percent).
